cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "One Law, and a Witness That Runs Backwards"
abstract: >-
  A synthesis of two independently-run hardware programmes, with one dedicated
  hardware run of its own. Both are shown to measure the same law: coherence surviving as
  a product of half-angle state overlaps, one factor per party that has
  acquired distinguishing information -- one programme measuring it against
  coupling angle, the other against time. Read on a marginal, that law produces
  an observable which is not merely uninformative about entanglement but
  suppressed by it, since for a bipartite pure state the marginal entropy is
  the entanglement entropy. A zero-entanglement product state therefore scores
  higher than a maximally entangled history state at every clock dimension, and
  the exact d=4 ratio of 4.24 reproduces the 4.2x measured on hardware in an
  adversarial run. Two exact structural nulls, one on a separable state and one
  on a maximally entangled state, follow from the same mechanism. Certification
  of quantum structure therefore requires agreement across incompatible
  measurements, a conclusion reached independently by both programmes and by a
  third line elsewhere. Includes the derived Holevo and relativistic
  storage/throughput bounds that answer the engineering questions the line
  began with.
type: software
version: 0.3.0
license: MIT
